WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:07.530
- My name's Zenna.

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:10.200
I'm a research scientist at Columbia,

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:13.170
and I run a small research
organization called Basis.

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:15.600
And today I'm gonna be
talking about inference

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.180
in causal probity programs
using an idea called

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.100
Non-standard Interpretation.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:21.150
And hopefully some

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:23.950
of these terms will become
clear by the end of the talk.

00:00:25.173 --> 00:00:28.277
So I'm gonna talk in three parts.

00:00:28.277 --> 00:00:30.030
The first thing I'm gonna talk about

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:32.460
is this kind of paradigm,
which is emerging

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.650
called causal public programming.

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:37.103
The second thing I'm gonna talk
about is program inversion.

00:00:37.103 --> 00:00:39.657
And then third, I'm gonna try
to tie these ideas together

00:00:39.657 --> 00:00:43.800
with this technology called
non-standard interpretation

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:45.279
or non-standard semantics.

00:00:45.279 --> 00:00:48.360
Many of the ideas that
have been discussed today,

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:49.500
I think about a lot.

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:52.154
They resonate very
heavily with my interests.

00:00:52.154 --> 00:00:55.902
This talk will be a little
bit more kind of grounded

00:00:55.902 --> 00:00:58.773
in the technology and the
systems that we're building.

00:01:02.411 --> 00:01:03.768
So the first kind of starting point

00:01:03.768 --> 00:01:05.850
is our generative models.

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:08.160
And so I was looking up
the term generative model,

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:09.420
and I found the earliest reference

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:14.019
to be from the 1950s and by
Chomsky and his colleagues,

00:01:14.019 --> 00:01:15.720
where they were trying to kind

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:18.840
of formalize this idea of
something which generates, right,

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:20.460
in other words, can generate language.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:22.710
And this term has stayed
with us for a while,

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:23.790
and it's been kind of reused

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:26.756
and repurposed in different
forms of AI and statistics.

00:01:26.756 --> 00:01:29.040
So I think many people here are familiar

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:30.660
with probabilistic graphical models

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:32.520
as a kind of generative model.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:34.530
And also more recently things in ai,

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:36.900
now sometimes called
generative ai, for example.

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:38.010
This image on the right hand side

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:41.673
is a gen Stan model, which
is generated from noise.

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:45.510
So when we look at the commonality

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:47.768
between these different representations,

00:01:47.768 --> 00:01:51.330
one thing that becomes
clear is that we can encode

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:52.740
all of them as programs, right?

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:55.620
We can write down a program
for a context, free grammar

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:57.290
or a probabilistic context, free grammar.

00:01:57.290 --> 00:02:00.390
We can write down a program
for a public graphical model.

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:01.470
We can write down a program

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:03.999
for a gen or a generative model.

00:02:03.999 --> 00:02:06.651
And so a recent idea,
perhaps 10, 15 years old,

00:02:06.651 --> 00:02:09.000
is to say, well, what if instead

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.013
of having these different
specific representations

00:02:11.013 --> 00:02:16.013
of models, we try to say
that any program, any kind

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:18.420
of thing that we can simulate,

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:20.220
can be a probabilistic model, right?

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:22.230
So think of all the things
that we can simulate now

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:24.930
in physics, in robotics, in the climate,

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:26.490
you know, in pandemics.

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:27.570
These are all things

00:02:27.570 --> 00:02:29.130
that we know how to simulate forwards.

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:31.484
But what if we could use
these as probabilistic models

00:02:31.484 --> 00:02:34.890
and use them for inference
so we could go backwards.

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:37.860
So retrodict us, I think a
term that came up earlier

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:40.483
or infer some later parameters
given some evidence.

00:02:40.483 --> 00:02:42.960
And so this idea has been formalized

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:45.840
and instantiated with
a few different tools.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:46.800
And here are some

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:49.950
of the examples like
Turing, Pyro, Gen and Stan.

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:51.540
And these are programming languages,

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:53.310
or they're kind of embedded
programming languages,

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:55.410
which you can use today.

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:57.573
And the basic idea is that you
sit down, you write a model,

00:02:57.573 --> 00:03:00.833
you encode your uncertainty,
and you ask your questions.

00:03:00.833 --> 00:03:03.598
And the goal of the system
is to answer those questions

00:03:03.598 --> 00:03:06.300
in a systematic way.

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:09.030
And I'll explain how this works in detail,

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:10.980
but this is some of the
kind of pre-existing work,

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:13.863
which we've been extended
with some ideas of causality.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:18.568
So to take an example, so
imagine you see this scene,

00:03:18.568 --> 00:03:21.480
you can think about what happened.

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:23.261
So you can retrodict into the past.

00:03:23.261 --> 00:03:26.130
You can also make some kind of prediction

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:28.590
of what would also happen into the future.

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:31.590
But you can also ask a
counterfactual question, which is,

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:33.450
for example, what would've happened

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:35.910
had this barrier not been here, right?

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:37.770
So I think most people would think, well,

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:39.570
if this barrier had not been here,

00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:40.920
the car probably would've tumbled down

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:42.403
the side of the cliff.

00:03:42.403 --> 00:03:45.486
So my interest as a cognitive scientist,

00:03:45.486 --> 00:03:47.310
is to understand the mental mechanisms

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:48.882
by which this takes place.

00:03:48.882 --> 00:03:52.410
And my interest as a
engineer is to build systems

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:54.460
that can automate this kind of reasoning.

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:57.990
And so the high level conceptual picture

00:03:57.990 --> 00:04:00.180
behind these counteracts
is like as follows.

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:02.370
So on the left hand side,
we might imagine some space

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:05.570
of possible worlds or some
space of possible trajectories.

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:07.230
And in the middle image

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:08.760
is what we do with
probabilistic conditioning.

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:11.244
So we add evidence, and this
updates our belief about

00:04:11.244 --> 00:04:14.073
the kind of the more
plausible trajectories

00:04:14.073 --> 00:04:15.939
of how the world evolved.

00:04:15.939 --> 00:04:19.022
And counterfactual is
an interesting construct

00:04:19.022 --> 00:04:21.230
which combines probabilistic conditioning

00:04:21.230 --> 00:04:22.803
with causal interventions.

00:04:22.803 --> 00:04:25.380
So it's basically saying,
well, given what I know,

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:26.751
which is a middle image,

00:04:26.751 --> 00:04:29.190
what would've happened
had we made this cut

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:30.754
or this change into the world?

00:04:30.754 --> 00:04:34.177
And you know, it's kind
of a strange concept

00:04:34.177 --> 00:04:37.830
because the counterfactual
world is kind of conditioned on

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:40.380
or affected by what actually
happened in the real world,

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:41.760
even though it doesn't really exist

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:43.010
in some meaningful sense.

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:47.850
And so, as has come up
earlier in the talk,

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:50.430
it's been a lot of work in
the recent decades of trying

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:51.818
to formalize this intuition,

00:04:51.818 --> 00:04:54.487
of our ability to ask
these what if questions

00:04:54.487 --> 00:04:56.310
and turn them into a kind

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:58.530
of a systematic mathematical framework.

00:04:58.530 --> 00:04:59.580
And one of the key ideas

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:01.664
behind this is the cause and intervention.

00:05:01.664 --> 00:05:04.890
So causal probably is
programming languages

00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:06.090
build upon this paradigm,

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:08.730
which we've seen in our conference,

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:11.100
in our workshop so far,
kind of the Palian approach

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:14.970
to structural causality by
instantiating a do operator,

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:17.340
an intervention operator in the context

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:19.380
of a programming language, right?

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:20.820
And so what this would allow us to do

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:22.691
is to do causal interventions

00:05:22.691 --> 00:05:26.040
and correspondingly
counterfactual inference,

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:27.721
not just in simple graphical models,

00:05:27.721 --> 00:05:30.780
but in any program that you can write down

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:32.330
in any program language, right?

00:05:33.295 --> 00:05:35.445
And so, conceptually
we have this operator,

00:05:35.445 --> 00:05:38.760
which we call do, and the
meaning is as follows.

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:43.425
So if I say, Y sub X equals
x given, do you know X

00:05:43.425 --> 00:05:45.090
is a scientist small X?

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:48.720
I think there's some kind of
syntax thing which is broken.

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:51.310
It should be an arrow,
not an exclamation mark.

00:05:51.310 --> 00:05:56.160
What this means is that
Y sub X is the value

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:58.150
of Y had X been set to small X

00:05:58.150 --> 00:06:02.643
instead of what it happened to be, right?

00:06:05.164 --> 00:06:09.090
So lemme just show you a
very simple, simple example.

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:12.090
So suppose we know that X equals two,

00:06:12.090 --> 00:06:15.204
and then I say Y equals two times X.

00:06:15.204 --> 00:06:17.831
So now I know that Y is four,

00:06:17.831 --> 00:06:21.810
and so this y sub X, which is
why given two x is assigned

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:24.360
to three has the value of six.

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:26.820
So it's the value of Y
you know, had X been set

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:28.260
to something different to

00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:30.330
what it was actually set to, right?

00:06:30.330 --> 00:06:31.860
So it's a very simple idea,

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:34.360
but this is at the foundation
of causal reasoning.

00:06:38.081 --> 00:06:41.250
And a counterfactual combines that ability

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:44.051
to do causal interventions with
probabilistic conditioning.

00:06:44.051 --> 00:06:46.017
So now I can say, you know, y give

00:06:46.017 --> 00:06:49.911
and do X goes to X conditioned
on evidence, right?

00:06:49.911 --> 00:06:52.547
So as we saw in my kind
of my previous diagram,

00:06:52.547 --> 00:06:55.953
we can incorporate data
back into this model.

00:06:58.530 --> 00:07:01.020
Okay, so that's the, there's
a very simple ingredient

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:03.330
and we've been building some systems

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:05.970
that take this idea and
instantiate it into a system

00:07:05.970 --> 00:07:09.300
that you can use to ask
very complex questions.

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:11.130
And so my PhD work was on kind

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:13.320
of the semantics and the idea behind this.

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:14.400
And now we've been building more

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:17.850
and more robust systems that
capture a kind of a wider

00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:20.190
and wider set of problems
that you might care about.

00:07:20.190 --> 00:07:22.016
And the most recent
system is called ChiRho.

00:07:22.016 --> 00:07:25.470
Very, very new, an output from basis.

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:26.700
And I recommend you go and check it out.

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:27.630
We've got lots of tutorials,

00:07:27.630 --> 00:07:29.580
which I'll go through one of right now.

00:07:31.350 --> 00:07:33.150
So the idea of a dynamical system

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:35.397
and time has come up a lot
in this conference so far.

00:07:35.397 --> 00:07:37.350
And the way that we approach this

00:07:37.350 --> 00:07:40.680
in kind of causal publicity
programming is to say, well,

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:42.480
let's take any kind of dynamical system

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:45.225
that you could express,
for example, an ODE model

00:07:45.225 --> 00:07:49.365
or a PDE, and let's make a
Bayesian version of that.

00:07:49.365 --> 00:07:51.420
And now let's do counterfactual inference

00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:53.070
with that kind of model.

00:07:53.070 --> 00:07:54.420
So what I'm showing you on the screen

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:55.950
is just a very small code snippet,

00:07:55.950 --> 00:07:57.574
which is encoding an SIR model,

00:07:57.574 --> 00:07:59.100
which is the kind of model we use

00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:02.011
in kind of pandemic
modeling where you've got

00:08:02.011 --> 00:08:06.390
S for susceptible, I for
infected, R for recovered

00:08:06.390 --> 00:08:08.552
to denote these three
different population sizes

00:08:08.552 --> 00:08:13.293
kind of evolving independently over time.

00:08:15.228 --> 00:08:18.150
And so this is what it would
look like a normal SIR model,

00:08:18.150 --> 00:08:20.280
the kind of, which, I'm sure many

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:23.190
of you have seen, and then
we've got some samples

00:08:23.190 --> 00:08:25.140
of data which we're gonna condition on.

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:28.320
So the next step in using

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:29.730
a causal probably programming language

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:30.780
is to make it bayesian.

00:08:30.780 --> 00:08:32.580
And what this means is
taking your parameters,

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:34.022
which are normally fixed

00:08:34.022 --> 00:08:37.020
and turning them into
distributions, right?

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:40.405
So this SIR model has a
beta and gamma parameter,

00:08:40.405 --> 00:08:42.990
and now instead of saying
that they're concrete numbers,

00:08:42.990 --> 00:08:46.380
we're gonna use, I think in
this case, a beta distribution

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:48.540
and a gamma distribution
to express our uncertainty

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:50.190
about what these values could be.

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:54.000
And what I'm showing you here

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:56.880
is just a visual
illustration of this, right?

00:08:56.880 --> 00:08:58.920
So we've got a confidence
interval showing kind

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:00.622
of a belief about the possible values

00:09:00.622 --> 00:09:03.052
that would kind of be implied

00:09:03.052 --> 00:09:06.780
over the trajectories
given these distributions

00:09:06.780 --> 00:09:08.550
over the parameters, right?

00:09:08.550 --> 00:09:10.767
So these very broad, kind of

00:09:10.767 --> 00:09:13.530
a wide shaded areas are
showing that there's lots

00:09:13.530 --> 00:09:16.430
of uncertainty about how the
system will evolve over time.

00:09:18.444 --> 00:09:20.280
And then the next thing you do

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:22.440
in a causal probabilistic program language

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:24.150
is that you just ask
your inference question.

00:09:24.150 --> 00:09:26.520
You say, well, given
this data, now tell me

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:28.740
what I should believe about
the future trajectories

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:30.323
of the system.

00:09:30.323 --> 00:09:33.030
And the inference system
will automatically solve

00:09:33.030 --> 00:09:33.900
that for you, right?

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:35.640
So this is, it's not zero code,

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:37.440
but it's a small amount of code compared

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:38.916
to what you'd have to do

00:09:38.916 --> 00:09:41.220
if you wanted to kind
of encode this by hand.

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:43.260
And the result is the
posterior distribution

00:09:43.260 --> 00:09:44.730
or the conditional distribution

00:09:44.730 --> 00:09:46.502
or the distribution given the evidence

00:09:46.502 --> 00:09:49.346
of what we should believe about
how the system would evolve

00:09:49.346 --> 00:09:51.360
given our data, right?

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:53.850
And as you can see from
this very kind of broad,

00:09:53.850 --> 00:09:55.020
high variance distribution,

00:09:55.020 --> 00:09:56.850
we now have a much tighter distribution.

00:09:56.850 --> 00:09:59.613
We're more informed about
how the world might evolve.

00:10:00.570 --> 00:10:02.430
So, so far I've spoken purely

00:10:02.430 --> 00:10:04.110
about probabilistic inference, right?

00:10:04.110 --> 00:10:05.363
I've taken a dynamical system,

00:10:05.363 --> 00:10:09.636
I've put some prize over the
parameters to make it Bayesian.

00:10:09.636 --> 00:10:11.700
I've used the publicity programming system

00:10:11.700 --> 00:10:13.710
to automate inference,

00:10:13.710 --> 00:10:15.270
and now I've got a belief about

00:10:15.270 --> 00:10:17.685
what's called a posterior
predictive distribution.

00:10:17.685 --> 00:10:20.414
But now we wanna go into causality

00:10:20.414 --> 00:10:22.110
and cause and interventions, right?

00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:23.370
So suppose you work in government

00:10:23.370 --> 00:10:25.608
and you wanna know the
impact of an intervention

00:10:25.608 --> 00:10:28.987
on the public, is your
lockdown policy going to work?

00:10:28.987 --> 00:10:31.710
And so the way that we can encode this

00:10:31.710 --> 00:10:33.533
is to basically impose

00:10:33.533 --> 00:10:37.961
an intervention over this
beta parameter, right?

00:10:37.961 --> 00:10:41.595
So we can have some
function of time encoding,

00:10:41.595 --> 00:10:44.530
like how much we're gonna force the value

00:10:45.645 --> 00:10:47.280
of beta to vary over time.

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:49.680
And this changes our
dynamical system a little bit.

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:53.640
And for some reason, a lot of
these methods got messed up.

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:55.530
So you're just gonna have to imagine this

00:10:55.530 --> 00:10:58.443
as a well-formed dynamical system.

00:10:59.383 --> 00:11:04.383
And then we basically write
a little bit of code, right?

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:08.370
To encode the intervention
that we want to express, right?

00:11:08.370 --> 00:11:09.510
And again, this is not no code,

00:11:09.510 --> 00:11:12.300
but it's a small amount of
code, and we're including all

00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:13.800
of the previous code that
we've already written.

00:11:13.800 --> 00:11:16.230
So there's this notion of
compositionally that we get.

00:11:16.230 --> 00:11:17.280
We don't have to rewrite everything

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:19.860
from scratch to consider
every new question

00:11:19.860 --> 00:11:20.730
that we might want to ask.

00:11:20.730 --> 00:11:22.890
In the same way as a
human, I don't have to,

00:11:22.890 --> 00:11:24.810
I can ask many different
questions given my state

00:11:24.810 --> 00:11:26.760
of knowledge about how the world works.

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:30.060
And so this is the
inference system turns away,

00:11:30.060 --> 00:11:31.110
and it gives us an inference

00:11:31.110 --> 00:11:32.610
about what would happen if we were

00:11:32.610 --> 00:11:35.730
to instantiate this
intervention into the world.

00:11:35.730 --> 00:11:38.580
And what we see by you can
see on the left hand side,

00:11:38.580 --> 00:11:41.220
we have this, they're susceptible, right?

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:43.440
And these two bars are showing the period

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:45.095
in which we're applying this intervention.

00:11:45.095 --> 00:11:47.653
You can see that it goes down,
you can see the infection,

00:11:47.653 --> 00:11:49.822
the infected rate stays
pretty much constant,

00:11:49.822 --> 00:11:52.320
and the recovery rate, you
know, increases, right?

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:53.714
So this has given us some indication

00:11:53.714 --> 00:11:56.670
that our lockdown policy
is going to work, right?

00:11:56.670 --> 00:11:58.286
And again, this is the causal intervention

00:11:58.286 --> 00:11:59.520
and it's the counterfactual

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:01.200
because it's conditional on the evidence

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:02.950
that we've seen so far in the world

00:12:05.598 --> 00:12:07.749
and we can make things
more complex, right?

00:12:07.749 --> 00:12:09.522
And this is one of the
really nice features.

00:12:09.522 --> 00:12:10.355
- A quick question.
- Yeah.

00:12:10.355 --> 00:12:12.120
- [Moderator] So these three
populations are considered

00:12:12.120 --> 00:12:13.470
to be completely distinct, right?

00:12:13.470 --> 00:12:14.670
With no overlaps

00:12:14.670 --> 00:12:19.260
and no movement from one to
the other over the timescale

00:12:19.260 --> 00:12:20.583
that you have down there?

00:12:21.510 --> 00:12:23.160
- Well, they're kind of correlated

00:12:23.160 --> 00:12:24.510
by the dynamical system.

00:12:24.510 --> 00:12:26.462
Yeah, so they're not independent,

00:12:26.462 --> 00:12:27.990
they're related in that sense.

00:12:27.990 --> 00:12:30.387
So they're all part of
the same model, yeah.

00:12:30.387 --> 00:12:34.140
But yeah, that's the most
basic kind of intervention.

00:12:34.140 --> 00:12:35.490
We chose some start point

00:12:35.490 --> 00:12:38.054
and some endpoint in which we
wanna apply this intervention.

00:12:38.054 --> 00:12:40.140
But as it's come up earlier in discussion,

00:12:40.140 --> 00:12:41.240
there's more complex ways

00:12:41.240 --> 00:12:42.750
we want to intervene into the world.

00:12:42.750 --> 00:12:44.611
And an intervention really
should be sort of as a model

00:12:44.611 --> 00:12:47.032
of how people might
interact with the world.

00:12:47.032 --> 00:12:49.740
And so, for example, we might not believe

00:12:49.740 --> 00:12:52.500
that if we decide to put in
place our policy at, you know,

00:12:52.500 --> 00:12:54.600
T equals one, that the world will respond

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:56.010
in the way that we want.

00:12:56.010 --> 00:12:57.720
And so one of the nice
things about a probabilistic

00:12:57.720 --> 00:13:00.360
programming language is that
we can just say, well actually,

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:02.460
well, let's just set this, this start time

00:13:02.460 --> 00:13:05.490
and this end time to be
unknown or uncertain, right?

00:13:05.490 --> 00:13:07.680
So we have some desire
to influence the world,

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:10.470
but we don't quite know when and how

00:13:10.470 --> 00:13:12.780
and to what degree our
policy is going to be adopted

00:13:12.780 --> 00:13:14.520
by society, right?

00:13:14.520 --> 00:13:16.019
And so this is just showing
the results of this kind

00:13:16.019 --> 00:13:18.840
of inference, and the results
are different, as you can see.

00:13:18.840 --> 00:13:20.551
There's more variants in the result.

00:13:20.551 --> 00:13:22.410
And the main point here

00:13:22.410 --> 00:13:25.110
is that to go from my simple intervention

00:13:25.110 --> 00:13:26.420
to a more complex intervention

00:13:26.420 --> 00:13:28.484
is a trivial change of code.

00:13:28.484 --> 00:13:31.075
Whereas, previously somebody would write

00:13:31.075 --> 00:13:32.070
an entire paper

00:13:32.070 --> 00:13:34.833
about this new kind of
counterfactual inference.

00:13:38.227 --> 00:13:40.680
And so, ChiRho as a system is very new,

00:13:40.680 --> 00:13:43.080
but we are kind of
steadily incorporating more

00:13:43.080 --> 00:13:44.730
and more kinds of counterfactual

00:13:44.730 --> 00:13:45.990
and causal reasoning that you might,

00:13:45.990 --> 00:13:46.980
that you might read in paper.

00:13:46.980 --> 00:13:48.480
And so we've got many examples

00:13:49.547 --> 00:13:50.640
with quite complex simulations,

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:53.100
assessing the causal
impact of genes of, sorry,

00:13:53.100 --> 00:13:55.233
of different therapies on genes.

00:13:55.233 --> 00:13:58.740
And the grand vision is to
incorporate the entire tool set

00:13:58.740 --> 00:14:00.510
of things that people
use in causal inference

00:14:00.510 --> 00:14:02.850
within a systematic
framework that is composable

00:14:02.850 --> 00:14:05.880
and can be extended to new models, right?

00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:08.330
And to new methods in a
very straightforward way.

00:14:11.610 --> 00:14:14.130
Okay, so one of the things
that arises when you work

00:14:14.130 --> 00:14:17.220
with simulation models is this kind

00:14:17.220 --> 00:14:19.320
of likelihood free inference regime.

00:14:19.320 --> 00:14:22.890
How do you condition simulation model?

00:14:22.890 --> 00:14:25.738
You've got some complex model,
it runs for a long time.

00:14:25.738 --> 00:14:28.350
And in some ways you
wanna constrict the output

00:14:28.350 --> 00:14:31.533
of this model to be consistent
with some data that you have.

00:14:32.543 --> 00:14:34.551
And so this is the problem

00:14:34.551 --> 00:14:35.745
that many people have thought about.

00:14:35.745 --> 00:14:37.830
If you are kind of very in the
weeds of probably inferences,

00:14:37.830 --> 00:14:40.470
things like A, B, C,
approximate basing computation

00:14:40.470 --> 00:14:42.600
and others, and we've been thinking about

00:14:42.600 --> 00:14:44.141
this from a programming perspective

00:14:44.141 --> 00:14:47.035
and from an inverse function perspective.

00:14:47.035 --> 00:14:48.930
So what is an inverse problem?

00:14:48.930 --> 00:14:51.630
Well, at a high level you've
got some forward function F

00:14:51.630 --> 00:14:53.749
and some output I, so sorry, output Y

00:14:53.749 --> 00:14:55.710
and you wanna find some input x

00:14:55.710 --> 00:14:57.540
such that F of X is equal to Y.

00:14:57.540 --> 00:15:00.440
That's a kind of an inverse
problem in the simplest sense.

00:15:01.650 --> 00:15:03.990
And so we can say like,
what is programming version?

00:15:03.990 --> 00:15:05.130
Well, it's the same thing, but instead

00:15:05.130 --> 00:15:06.660
of just thinking about
mathematical functions,

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:07.800
we're gonna talk about programs

00:15:07.800 --> 00:15:08.820
that compute those functions.

00:15:08.820 --> 00:15:10.350
So you've got some simulator

00:15:10.350 --> 00:15:12.540
and it's got some output that we know

00:15:12.540 --> 00:15:15.090
and we wanna find the inputs
that are consistent with that.

00:15:15.090 --> 00:15:17.030
So let me give you two examples

00:15:17.030 --> 00:15:20.970
why we should care about this.

00:15:20.970 --> 00:15:22.920
So one is in just an inference in general.

00:15:22.920 --> 00:15:26.010
So I suppose, well not
suppose, no, the real world

00:15:26.010 --> 00:15:27.510
is three dimensional, right?

00:15:27.510 --> 00:15:29.940
I think we can mostly agree on that.

00:15:29.940 --> 00:15:32.400
But what we observe as humans
is two dimensional, right?

00:15:32.400 --> 00:15:34.470
Our eyes are basically cameras.

00:15:34.470 --> 00:15:35.610
And so somehow as humans,

00:15:35.610 --> 00:15:37.500
we have the ability to look into the world

00:15:37.500 --> 00:15:39.030
and figure out the three
dimensional structure

00:15:39.030 --> 00:15:41.684
of the world given our
two dimensional input.

00:15:41.684 --> 00:15:44.550
And so you can think about this

00:15:44.550 --> 00:15:46.380
as an inverse problem, right?

00:15:46.380 --> 00:15:48.810
Because we understand the forward process,

00:15:48.810 --> 00:15:50.670
which goes from the
three dimensional world

00:15:50.670 --> 00:15:51.720
to a two dimensional image

00:15:51.720 --> 00:15:54.090
that's sometimes called
rendering or computer graphics.

00:15:54.090 --> 00:15:55.290
That's what powers our video games

00:15:55.290 --> 00:15:56.490
and the animations we see.

00:15:56.490 --> 00:15:58.680
But what we want is the inverse of that.

00:15:58.680 --> 00:16:00.495
We wanna go from the 2D to the 3D.

00:16:00.495 --> 00:16:03.300
So what if we could invert
this rendering algorithm?

00:16:03.300 --> 00:16:05.610
It would then be an
inference algorithm, right?

00:16:05.610 --> 00:16:07.230
Or in robotics and control, right?

00:16:07.230 --> 00:16:09.630
We have a forward kinematic
function, which tells us,

00:16:09.630 --> 00:16:11.820
if I know the kind of the
joint angles of a robot,

00:16:11.820 --> 00:16:13.047
it will tell me where the hand is.

00:16:13.047 --> 00:16:15.150
But what I want is the
inverse of that to say, well,

00:16:15.150 --> 00:16:16.830
where should I put my
joint angles in order

00:16:16.830 --> 00:16:18.430
to get my hand to this position?

00:16:21.420 --> 00:16:22.890
And so the first challenge that you'll see

00:16:22.890 --> 00:16:25.186
is basically the non
invert of these functions,

00:16:25.186 --> 00:16:29.370
almost any interesting function
that you want to kind of use

00:16:29.370 --> 00:16:31.025
for inference is not invertible.

00:16:31.025 --> 00:16:36.025
And so in the case of
this kind of graphics

00:16:36.480 --> 00:16:37.710
and rendering, like why is that?

00:16:37.710 --> 00:16:39.450
Well, one simple reason
is occlusion, right?

00:16:39.450 --> 00:16:42.300
If I had some green ball behind my back,

00:16:42.300 --> 00:16:43.710
you would see the same thing.

00:16:43.710 --> 00:16:45.690
And so there's ambiguity about

00:16:45.690 --> 00:16:48.360
what is the true reality conditional

00:16:48.360 --> 00:16:50.970
on the evidence that you've observed.

00:16:50.970 --> 00:16:52.920
Or even in the case of
something as simple as addition,

00:16:52.920 --> 00:16:55.787
if I tell you that X
plus Y is equal to five,

00:16:55.787 --> 00:16:58.871
you don't know what x and Y
are, you could be one in three,

00:16:58.871 --> 00:17:00.399
sorry, one in four, I can't add,

00:17:00.399 --> 00:17:04.230
it could be zero and five, right?

00:17:04.230 --> 00:17:06.120
There's an infinite set of combinations

00:17:06.120 --> 00:17:07.803
which sum to five.

00:17:09.780 --> 00:17:11.940
And so the basic approach that I took

00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:15.240
to this problem is an idea
we call parametric inversion.

00:17:15.240 --> 00:17:17.310
And this, it is unfortunate
here that the math has got

00:17:17.310 --> 00:17:19.800
so messed up because it does matter.

00:17:19.800 --> 00:17:22.996
But the basic idea is to
define a generalization

00:17:22.996 --> 00:17:24.811
of the notion of an inverse.

00:17:24.811 --> 00:17:29.811
And so, if the conceptual understanding

00:17:31.650 --> 00:17:34.115
is that for any given
output of a function,

00:17:34.115 --> 00:17:37.290
let's say addition,
there's, as we just said,

00:17:37.290 --> 00:17:38.370
there's a set of possible inputs

00:17:38.370 --> 00:17:40.410
that map to that same output.

00:17:40.410 --> 00:17:42.510
And let's try to parametrically
represent that set.

00:17:42.510 --> 00:17:44.270
So we're gonna define a new function

00:17:44.270 --> 00:17:46.350
of which we call a parametric inverse,

00:17:46.350 --> 00:17:48.510
which takes a value in the output

00:17:48.510 --> 00:17:50.812
of the original function
and goes backwards.

00:17:50.812 --> 00:17:54.030
But it uses a parameter to kind of select

00:17:54.030 --> 00:17:55.440
between the different possible inputs

00:17:55.440 --> 00:17:56.840
that map to the same output.

00:18:00.510 --> 00:18:01.980
So what is the high level idea?

00:18:01.980 --> 00:18:03.720
So the high level idea,
the high level goal

00:18:03.720 --> 00:18:04.820
is that you write down a program

00:18:04.820 --> 00:18:06.120
in your favorite programming language,

00:18:06.120 --> 00:18:08.602
whether that's Python
or Julia or whatever.

00:18:08.602 --> 00:18:10.943
Now instead of running
that program forward,

00:18:10.943 --> 00:18:13.230
you can run it backwards, right?

00:18:13.230 --> 00:18:14.550
So you can say, well, I know the output

00:18:14.550 --> 00:18:15.930
and I know some parameters

00:18:15.930 --> 00:18:17.940
and that's gonna allow me to
run this program backwards

00:18:17.940 --> 00:18:20.663
and choose one of the inputs
that map to the same output.

00:18:22.410 --> 00:18:23.400
So lemme just give you an example

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:25.140
of what a parametric inverse is.

00:18:25.140 --> 00:18:28.439
So take addition, as
we've been using X plus,

00:18:28.439 --> 00:18:30.326
let's say Z equals X plus Y

00:18:30.326 --> 00:18:34.800
or F of X equals x plus Y, F
of x and Y equals x plus y.

00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:38.340
A parametric inverse of this
function will take in a value Z

00:18:38.340 --> 00:18:40.290
take in a parameter theta,

00:18:40.290 --> 00:18:43.155
and it will return two
values, one for X, one for Y,

00:18:43.155 --> 00:18:47.222
and those values will be
Z minus theta and theta.

00:18:47.222 --> 00:18:51.180
So the point is that Z
minus theta plus theta

00:18:51.180 --> 00:18:52.647
is equal to Z, right?

00:18:52.647 --> 00:18:55.020
And the other point is
that as I vary theta,

00:18:55.020 --> 00:18:56.700
I'm getting back all the
different combinations

00:18:56.700 --> 00:18:59.910
of X and Y which map two Z, right?

00:18:59.910 --> 00:19:02.697
So now I've got a way
to generalize inversion

00:19:02.697 --> 00:19:05.940
for a non invertible
function that allows me

00:19:05.940 --> 00:19:07.740
to parametrically select
the different inputs

00:19:07.740 --> 00:19:09.533
that map to the same output.

00:19:09.533 --> 00:19:11.820
And I can do the same
for the square function.

00:19:11.820 --> 00:19:13.470
And if you think about this,
you could do it the same

00:19:13.470 --> 00:19:16.533
for many of your favorite
functions that you use every day.

00:19:17.891 --> 00:19:22.891
Okay, so I'll skip this aside
because it's too hard to read,

00:19:23.190 --> 00:19:25.683
but that's the basic definition.

00:19:28.462 --> 00:19:30.630
Okay, so you know, what we've seen

00:19:30.630 --> 00:19:32.490
is that we can take simple
functions like addition

00:19:32.490 --> 00:19:33.930
or square root or square function,

00:19:33.930 --> 00:19:35.550
and we can define these
parametric inverses,

00:19:35.550 --> 00:19:37.430
which generalize the notion of inversion.

00:19:37.430 --> 00:19:40.134
But our goal is to invert
complicated simulation models.

00:19:40.134 --> 00:19:43.500
And so the key idea behind
parametric inversion

00:19:43.500 --> 00:19:44.520
is composition.

00:19:44.520 --> 00:19:46.542
And that is any complicated model

00:19:46.542 --> 00:19:49.920
or any complicated program
ultimately reduces down

00:19:49.920 --> 00:19:52.590
to a small set of primitives
composed together.

00:19:52.590 --> 00:19:53.880
That's how programming languages work.

00:19:53.880 --> 00:19:55.800
That's what allows us to write programs

00:19:55.800 --> 00:19:57.141
of millions of lines of code.

00:19:57.141 --> 00:20:00.729
And so the basic algorithm
behind parametric inversion

00:20:00.729 --> 00:20:02.970
is to take a composition,
and we'll just start

00:20:02.970 --> 00:20:05.607
with the composition of
two functions, addition

00:20:05.607 --> 00:20:07.142
and a square function, right?

00:20:07.142 --> 00:20:10.080
And we'll take that addition

00:20:10.080 --> 00:20:12.000
and we'll replace it with
its parametric inverse one

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:13.560
that we just defined.

00:20:13.560 --> 00:20:14.850
And we'll take the square function

00:20:14.850 --> 00:20:17.310
and we'll replace that with
its parametric inverse,

00:20:17.310 --> 00:20:19.763
and then we'll reverse the
order of the composition.

00:20:20.610 --> 00:20:24.438
So that procedure is a
mechanism to turn a program

00:20:24.438 --> 00:20:28.351
into its parametric inverse, right?

00:20:28.351 --> 00:20:33.351
- Okay, and this is just
saying that in words,

00:20:33.720 --> 00:20:36.420
but this is in some
ways a very large claim.

00:20:36.420 --> 00:20:37.800
Like it maybe sounds simple,

00:20:37.800 --> 00:20:39.210
but I'm basically arguing

00:20:39.210 --> 00:20:40.852
that you can have a mechanical procedure

00:20:40.852 --> 00:20:44.130
to invert arbitrary
arbitrarily complex programs.

00:20:44.130 --> 00:20:47.460
And if you can do such a thing,
it vastly expands the space

00:20:47.460 --> 00:20:49.800
of inference methods that we can develop

00:20:49.800 --> 00:20:52.710
and the space of problems that
we can solve in this paradigm

00:20:52.710 --> 00:20:54.720
of simulation based inference.

00:20:54.720 --> 00:20:56.502
So you should be skeptical,

00:20:56.502 --> 00:20:59.966
and the reason you should be skeptical

00:20:59.966 --> 00:21:02.373
is kind of explained by this example.

00:21:04.380 --> 00:21:06.900
So if I have this slightly
more complex function, right,

00:21:06.900 --> 00:21:11.370
which is just F of X and Y,
FX and Y equals x, y plus x,

00:21:11.370 --> 00:21:15.880
and I'm using this green
function to just duplicate Y

00:21:15.880 --> 00:21:19.410
before adding it, one of the issues

00:21:19.410 --> 00:21:22.740
that arises is that if
I try to go backwards,

00:21:22.740 --> 00:21:24.930
I apply the procedures
that I just described.

00:21:24.930 --> 00:21:27.330
I've taken the multiplication,
I've inverted that,

00:21:27.330 --> 00:21:29.025
I've taken the addition,
I've inverted that

00:21:29.025 --> 00:21:31.437
and I've taken this
duplication and inverted that,

00:21:31.437 --> 00:21:33.385
and I've run the whole thing backwards.

00:21:33.385 --> 00:21:35.040
One of the issues that comes up

00:21:35.040 --> 00:21:38.555
is there are some constraints
on the parameter spaces

00:21:38.555 --> 00:21:40.620
that arise on the addition

00:21:40.620 --> 00:21:42.330
and the multiplication that can lead

00:21:42.330 --> 00:21:45.120
to inconsistencies if you
choose the wrong things.

00:21:45.120 --> 00:21:49.580
In other words, the
parametric inverse is correct,

00:21:49.580 --> 00:21:51.724
but you might put in some parameter values

00:21:51.724 --> 00:21:52.920
which are invalid.

00:21:52.920 --> 00:21:54.393
So it's a partial function.

00:21:55.722 --> 00:21:59.250
So the procedure that I just described,

00:21:59.250 --> 00:22:00.990
everything I've told you so far is true.

00:22:00.990 --> 00:22:03.150
We have a mechanical
procedure, an algorithm

00:22:03.150 --> 00:22:05.040
to invert programs,

00:22:05.040 --> 00:22:07.950
but it could be the
case that it's undefined

00:22:07.950 --> 00:22:10.065
as some of the parameter values.

00:22:10.065 --> 00:22:13.260
And so, why does this occur?

00:22:13.260 --> 00:22:14.970
And I think it actually
relates heavily to some

00:22:14.970 --> 00:22:18.090
of the more philosophical
things that have come up today,

00:22:18.090 --> 00:22:19.410
which is that in the four direction,

00:22:19.410 --> 00:22:21.345
there's really just like one way to go.

00:22:21.345 --> 00:22:23.430
And in the backwards direction,

00:22:23.430 --> 00:22:25.748
there's many choices
that you have to make.

00:22:25.748 --> 00:22:28.200
It's almost like every
computational operation,

00:22:28.200 --> 00:22:30.480
you're making a choice about what to do,

00:22:30.480 --> 00:22:32.610
and your parameter value is choosing that.

00:22:32.610 --> 00:22:34.740
And you can choose some parts
which are just impossible

00:22:34.740 --> 00:22:36.150
in the forward direction, right?

00:22:36.150 --> 00:22:37.890
And if you do so, you'll get stuck

00:22:37.890 --> 00:22:40.090
and you'll kind of be
doing the wrong thing.

00:22:43.680 --> 00:22:46.980
And so, this gives us a kind of a paradigm

00:22:46.980 --> 00:22:48.432
for thinking about inference.

00:22:48.432 --> 00:22:52.290
So normally if you think about
black box based inference,

00:22:52.290 --> 00:22:54.870
I've got some model
and I wanna find some X

00:22:54.870 --> 00:22:56.940
such that FX equal to Y,

00:22:56.940 --> 00:22:58.860
I could do some search over the space of X

00:22:58.860 --> 00:23:00.897
and I could check, you
know, is this equal to Y?

00:23:00.897 --> 00:23:01.980
And I could do some check,

00:23:01.980 --> 00:23:04.200
and if I get close enough, I'll be happy.

00:23:04.200 --> 00:23:06.064
That's how approximate computation works.

00:23:06.064 --> 00:23:07.920
With parametric inversion,

00:23:07.920 --> 00:23:09.090
you have a kind of a different problem,

00:23:09.090 --> 00:23:09.930
which you actually start

00:23:09.930 --> 00:23:11.820
with the output, you run it backwards,

00:23:11.820 --> 00:23:13.260
but you might fall into
a different problem,

00:23:13.260 --> 00:23:16.160
which is that your parameters
are not quite what you want.

00:23:17.910 --> 00:23:19.740
And so we put a lot of
effort into trying to solve

00:23:19.740 --> 00:23:22.294
that problem and to just say

00:23:22.294 --> 00:23:25.380
our approach very briefly,
we use machine learning

00:23:25.380 --> 00:23:27.540
to help us find good parts
of this parameter space.

00:23:27.540 --> 00:23:30.572
And it turns out that actually
we can generate data sets

00:23:30.572 --> 00:23:32.130
of inputs, outputs, and parameters,

00:23:32.130 --> 00:23:34.307
and use machine learning models

00:23:34.307 --> 00:23:35.850
to help us navigate this
parameter space to choose good,

00:23:35.850 --> 00:23:38.050
to kind of find the
good parts of the space.

00:23:41.010 --> 00:23:42.390
And also more broadly, and again, related

00:23:42.390 --> 00:23:45.300
to some of the more philosophical issues,

00:23:45.300 --> 00:23:46.770
it turns out that you
don't actually just have

00:23:46.770 --> 00:23:47.910
to go forward or backwards.

00:23:47.910 --> 00:23:50.356
You can actually navigate in
almost arbitrary direction

00:23:50.356 --> 00:23:52.920
inside the structure of a model.

00:23:52.920 --> 00:23:55.080
And this really kind of
changes how you think about

00:23:55.080 --> 00:23:56.507
what a program is, right?

00:23:56.507 --> 00:23:58.800
A program is really defining relationships

00:23:58.800 --> 00:24:01.260
between different parts of things

00:24:01.260 --> 00:24:04.796
that you care about and how
you kind of execute that

00:24:04.796 --> 00:24:07.710
or kind of run that there's a lot

00:24:07.710 --> 00:24:09.710
of flexibility in what you decide to do.

00:24:11.820 --> 00:24:13.650
Okay, I'm gonna step back
and just quickly take

00:24:13.650 --> 00:24:16.560
a broader view, which is that many modes

00:24:16.560 --> 00:24:18.720
of reasoning can be
understood as different kinds

00:24:18.720 --> 00:24:20.313
of program transformations.

00:24:22.388 --> 00:24:24.240
But an even more basic question

00:24:24.240 --> 00:24:25.860
is what is a program, right?

00:24:25.860 --> 00:24:28.819
So I'm sure everybody or most
people here have programmed,

00:24:28.819 --> 00:24:31.290
but the way that we think
about programming languages

00:24:31.290 --> 00:24:32.928
in kind of the theoretical community

00:24:32.928 --> 00:24:35.940
is that a program has some syntax, right?

00:24:35.940 --> 00:24:37.590
Some kind of well structured form

00:24:37.590 --> 00:24:41.280
of writing programs and it has
some semantics, some meaning

00:24:41.280 --> 00:24:42.249
to those programs

00:24:42.249 --> 00:24:44.760
and it has some implementation, a compiler

00:24:44.760 --> 00:24:47.460
or an interpreter that
actually runs those programs.

00:24:47.460 --> 00:24:49.440
And the way that PR people kind

00:24:49.440 --> 00:24:51.480
of think about programs is
they write these little tables

00:24:51.480 --> 00:24:53.763
and rule systems to
define what programs are.

00:24:55.947 --> 00:24:58.710
And program transformations
are in my view,

00:24:58.710 --> 00:24:59.880
this kind of hidden technology,

00:24:59.880 --> 00:25:02.114
which is powering the entire
machine learning revolution.

00:25:02.114 --> 00:25:04.472
So think about automatic
differentiation, right?

00:25:04.472 --> 00:25:07.318
Which is essentially
how deep learning works.

00:25:07.318 --> 00:25:08.640
Well, what is it?

00:25:08.640 --> 00:25:09.660
It's a program transformation.

00:25:09.660 --> 00:25:11.460
You're taking some program and you convert

00:25:11.460 --> 00:25:13.650
that into a program that
computes the gradient, right?

00:25:13.650 --> 00:25:15.458
You don't do this, some system does this,

00:25:15.458 --> 00:25:16.980
you know, you just write
torch stock, right?

00:25:16.980 --> 00:25:18.718
Or whatever, a high level thing.

00:25:18.718 --> 00:25:20.370
But in the background, there's some system

00:25:20.370 --> 00:25:22.021
which is transforming
the underlying program

00:25:22.021 --> 00:25:25.383
to turn a function into its derivative.

00:25:28.020 --> 00:25:32.580
So my narrower, broader view
is that a lot of the things

00:25:32.580 --> 00:25:34.350
that we want reasoning systems to do

00:25:34.350 --> 00:25:36.536
can be understood not just
as program transformations,

00:25:36.536 --> 00:25:40.800
but as what you might call
semantics transformations

00:25:40.800 --> 00:25:42.674
or non-standard interpretations.

00:25:42.674 --> 00:25:46.500
And going back to the example
that I had at the start

00:25:46.500 --> 00:25:49.040
with two times X and so on,

00:25:49.040 --> 00:25:51.390
the way that this is actually formalized

00:25:51.390 --> 00:25:54.578
is as a transformation to this semantics

00:25:54.578 --> 00:25:55.860
of this programming language, right?

00:25:55.860 --> 00:25:59.340
So this dual operator is
taking the normal meaning

00:25:59.340 --> 00:26:01.339
of a program and changing it in a way such

00:26:01.339 --> 00:26:04.061
that it makes sense to
do this kind of backwards

00:26:04.061 --> 00:26:07.473
modification of the kind
of the parent variables.

00:26:10.590 --> 00:26:14.262
And so, there's been this kind
of emerging paradigm of tools

00:26:14.262 --> 00:26:19.262
like JAX, Cassette, Enzyme,
which are allowing us as kind

00:26:19.440 --> 00:26:22.500
of inference method designers
to implement these kind

00:26:22.500 --> 00:26:25.860
of program transformations
within the same language.

00:26:25.860 --> 00:26:28.080
And it's opened up
automatic differentiation.

00:26:28.080 --> 00:26:30.150
It's how parametric
inversion is implemented,

00:26:30.150 --> 00:26:33.270
it's how cause of
interventions are implemented.

00:26:33.270 --> 00:26:34.470
And I expect many

00:26:34.470 --> 00:26:37.200
of the more advanced inference
methods going forward will

00:26:37.200 --> 00:26:38.403
rely on this technology.

00:26:39.990 --> 00:26:42.900
Okay, so to conclude, causal
probabilistic programming

00:26:42.900 --> 00:26:46.146
a is a new paradigm that
allows you to encode programs

00:26:46.146 --> 00:26:49.260
or models as programs
and automates inference

00:26:49.260 --> 00:26:51.570
so that you can just ask the
questions that you care about.

00:26:51.570 --> 00:26:53.280
And at least in the idealized world,

00:26:53.280 --> 00:26:56.220
it automatically computes
the answers for you.

00:26:56.220 --> 00:26:58.020
Programming version is at the heart

00:26:58.020 --> 00:26:59.040
of simulation based inference

00:26:59.040 --> 00:27:01.140
because somehow you need
to find inputs that map

00:27:01.140 --> 00:27:02.133
to a given output.

00:27:03.060 --> 00:27:04.650
The next generation of inference

00:27:04.650 --> 00:27:05.880
methods will not be black box.

00:27:05.880 --> 00:27:08.190
And by black box, I mean just
using the input and output,

00:27:08.190 --> 00:27:09.210
they're gonna introspect

00:27:09.210 --> 00:27:12.070
or inspect into the
internal structure of models

00:27:12.070 --> 00:27:15.123
and they'll rely on program
transformations to do so.

00:27:16.710 --> 00:27:18.900
And finally there's an emerging class

00:27:18.900 --> 00:27:21.731
of tools which are
starting to mechanize this,

00:27:21.731 --> 00:27:25.500
which I think everyone
should just be aware of.

00:27:25.500 --> 00:27:26.940
There are many challenges
and many difficult things,

00:27:26.940 --> 00:27:28.064
which I'll be happy to talk about,

00:27:28.064 --> 00:27:30.840
but this is the end of my talk

00:27:30.840 --> 00:27:32.460
and this is some of the
stuff that we're working on

00:27:32.460 --> 00:27:36.450
at Basis, which is a new
organization to do just this.

00:27:36.450 --> 00:27:37.646
So thank you.

